## Project Workflow

1. Load the customer dataset using Pandas.
2. Explore the dataset and inspect missing values.
3. Handle missing numerical values using mean imputation.
4. Handle missing categorical values using mode imputation.
5. Encode categorical variables using Label Encoding.
6. Split the dataset into 80% training data and 20% testing data.
7. Apply feature scaling using StandardScaler.
8. Train a Random Forest Classifier with 100 estimators.
9. Evaluate the model using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, and a confusion matrix.
10. Generate a purchase prediction and probability estimate for a new customer.

## Model Performance

The Random Forest Classifier achieved the following results on the test dataset:

| Metric | Result |
|---|---:|
| Accuracy | 91.82% |
| Precision — Non-buyers (Class 0) | 0.91 |
| Recall — Non-buyers (Class 0) | 0.99 |
| F1-score — Non-buyers (Class 0) | 0.95 |
| Precision — Buyers (Class 1) | 0.94 |
| Recall — Buyers (Class 1) | 0.62 |
| F1-score — Buyers (Class 1) | 0.75 |

**Key observation:** Although the model achieved 91.82% accuracy, its recall for actual buyers was 62%. This indicates an opportunity to improve the identification of potential customers.

## Confusion Matrix

| | Predicted: Not Purchased | Predicted: Purchased |
|---|---:|---:|
| Actual: Not Purchased | 780 | 7 |
| Actual: Purchased | 73 | 118 |

The model correctly identified 118 actual buyers while missing 73 actual buyers in the test dataset.

